.TH cron "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
cron \- execute commands at preset times
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/cron &\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
cron -- execute commands at preset times
Usage:	/etc/cron &
cron takes no command-line options or arguments.  It is normally
started once, from /etc/rc, and runs in the background forever.
.HE
.B cron
is a daemon that runs commands at preset times.
It takes no command-line arguments; it is normally started once, from
.BR /etc/rc ,
and never exits.
.PP
After starting,
.B cron
sets its user and group ids to those of
.B daemon
(if that user is found in the password file), changes its working
directory to
.BR /bin ,
and enters a loop that wakes once every minute.
Each time it wakes, it reopens
.B /usr/lib/crontab
and reads it one entry at a time, comparing the entry's time fields
against the current time.
.PP
Each entry begins with five time fields, separated by blanks:
.DS
.ta 0.3i 1.6i
	\fIminute\fR	0-59
	\fIhour\fR	0-23
	\fIday of month\fR	1-31
	\fImonth\fR	1-12
	\fIday of week\fR	0-6 (0 is Sunday)
.DE
Each field is either a lone
.BR * ,
matching every value; a single number; a range such as
\fI8\fR\-\fI17\fR;
or a comma-separated list of numbers and ranges.
The rest of the entry, following the fifth field, is the command to be
executed.
.PP
When every field of an entry matches the current minute, hour, day of
month, month, and day of week,
.B cron
runs the entry's command by piping it to a new shell.
A command may be continued onto more than one line of the entry by
ending each line but the last with a percent sign; before handing the
text to the shell,
.B cron
turns each such percent sign into a newline.
.PP
.B cron
ignores the signals
.BR SIGHUP ,
.BR SIGINT ,
and
.BR SIGPIPE ,
and uses
.B SIGALRM
internally to wake itself once a minute.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.6i
\fB/usr/lib/crontab\fR	table of commands to run
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR at ,
.B sh
.SH NOTES
.B cron
takes no options or arguments; any given on its command line are
ignored.
It does not keep a log of the commands it has run.
